# === Engine: MonoGame — undo the Unity block's project-file exclusions ===
# The "Engine: Unity" section above ignores *.csproj / *.sln. That is correct for Unity,
# which regenerates project files from its own asset database. It is WRONG for MonoGame:
# here the .csproj IS the project definition (target framework, NuGet refs, the pinned
# MonoGame 3.8.4.1 version, the MGCB content-pipeline hook). Without it, a fresh clone
# does not build at all. Re-include them.
!*.csproj
!*.sln
!*.slnx

# === Source directories the Unity build-output rules were eating ===
# `[Bb]uilds/` above (line ~53) is a Unity convention for compiled output. In THIS codebase
# `src/IdleXIdle.Core/Builds/` is source — the character build system — and the rule
# swallowed it silently: the files sat on disk, the tests passed, `git status` said "clean",
# and a fresh clone would not have compiled. Same class of bug as the `*.csproj` rule above.
#
# Re-include source, and keep ignoring anything that is actually build output.
!src/**/Builds/
!tests/**/Builds/

# Asset pipeline staging (raw generator output + logs)
tools/asset-pipeline/.staging/

# PixelLab API token (secret - never commit)
tools/asset-pipeline/.env

# Dev screenshot captures (tools/asset-pipeline/capture.sh) — regenerable, not source.
#
# THIS PREFIX IS THE POLICY, and production/qa/evidence/ relies on it. coding-standards.md wants a
# screenshot on file for every Visual/Feel and UI story, and the working rule that emerged is: a
# capture taken to LOOK at something is named shot_* and is ignored wherever it lands, including
# inside the evidence directory; a capture KEPT as the evidence for a story is given a real name
# (armour_tiers.png, roster_designs.png) and is committed. So "put it in evidence/" is not what makes
# something evidence — naming it is. Both of the tracked files got there that way.
#
# The alternative — negating the rule for evidence/ — was considered and rejected: that directory
# currently holds 21 files and 15 MB, and nineteen of them are this week's iteration captures. A
# trail that keeps everything is not a trail.
shot_*.png

# Verification captures written by audit agents — never source.
v_*.png
audit_*.png

# Filmstrip captures (tools/asset-pipeline/capture_seq.sh) — regenerable, not source.
seq_*.png

# The clip pipeline caches its fetched source frames beside each strip so a re-run after a
# network stall does not pay for the downloads again (tools/asset-pipeline/v2/clip.py). They are
# inputs, not assets: the strip beside them is what ships.
*.png.frames/
build/
